\title[EML Transseries]{EML Transseries: A Hahn-Series Model, the Asymptotic Comparison Theorem, and the Exp-Substitution Automorphism}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 27, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Power series cannot express the asymptotic relationship between a variable and its
exponential: no power-series valuation can encode an object dominating $x^{a}$ for every
real $a$ simultaneously. \emph{Transseries} repair this by enlarging the monomial basis to
formal products of iterated exponentials and logarithms with real exponents. We present a
rigorous, machine-checked model of single-tower, real-power transseries built on Hahn series
over the lexicographically ordered group of transmonomials $\Lex(\Z \to_{f} \R)$. We prove
this model is a field, that its valuation realizes asymptotic dominance (in particular that
$e^{x}$ dominates every power of $x$), and we establish the \emph{asymptotic comparison
theorem}: two transseries agreeing to all orders are equal, so a transseries is uniquely
determined by its expansion. Our central contribution is the \emph{exp-substitution
automorphism}: the operation $x \mapsto e^{x}$ is realized as an injective ring homomorphism
$\expShift\colon \TSeries \to \TSeries$ raising every tower height by one, fixing the
constant field $\R$, with $\expShift(x) = e^{x}$. The load-bearing lemma is that the height
shift preserves the dominance order, $\shift(x) < \shift(y) \iff x < y$, proved via the
behavior of lexicographic comparison under a monotone relabeling of indices.
\end{abstract}

