\section{Introduction}

A foundational asymptotic fact is that $x^{a} = o(e^{x})$ as $x \to \infty$ for every real
$a$. Iterating, $(e^{x})^{a} = o(e^{e^{x}})$, and dually $\log x$ is dominated by every
positive power of $x$. These facts organize the elementary functions into a doubly-infinite
hierarchy of \emph{tower heights},
\[
  \cdots \prec \log\log x \prec \log x \prec x \prec e^{x} \prec e^{e^{x}} \prec \cdots,
\]
indexed by $h \in \Z$ (height $0 = x$, height $1 = e^{x}$, height $-1 = \log x$, height
$2 = e^{e^{x}}$).

Power series cannot describe this hierarchy: their valuation is a single (integer or real)
number, and no such valuation admits an element dominating $x^{a}$ for \emph{all} $a$.
Transseries, developed by Dahn--G\"oring, \'Ecalle, van den Dries--Macintyre--Marker, and
Aschenbrenner--van den Dries--van der Hoeven, resolve this by allowing formal series whose
monomials are products of iterated exp/log with real exponents.

We give a self-contained, formally verified development in three layers: a field model
(\S\ref{sec:field}), the asymptotic comparison theorem (\S\ref{sec:comparison}), and the
exp-substitution automorphism (\S\ref{sec:expshift}).

\section{Preliminaries: Hahn series}

Let $\Gamma$ be a linearly ordered abelian group and $K$ a field. A \emph{Hahn series} over
$\Gamma$ with coefficients in $K$ is a function $f\colon \Gamma \to K$ whose support
$\{\gamma : f(\gamma) \neq 0\}$ is well-ordered. Hahn series form a ring under pointwise
addition and convolution multiplication; when $K$ is a field they form a field. The
valuation $\orderTop$ sends a nonzero series to the least element of its support and $0$ to
$\top$; it is multiplicative. We use the one-term series $\mathrm{single}\,g\,c$, the
constant embedding $\C$, and the order-embedding ring homomorphism $\mathrm{embDomainRingHom}$,
which lifts an injective, order-reflecting additive group homomorphism of the value group to
a ring homomorphism of the Hahn-series field.

\section{The field of transmonomials and transseries}\label{sec:field}

\begin{definition}[Transmonomials]
The \emph{transmonomial group} is $\TransMono := \Lex(\Z \to_{f} \R)$, the group of finitely
supported functions $\Z \to \R$ under pointwise addition with the lexicographic order. As
$\mathrm{Finsupp.Lex}$ compares at the \emph{least} differing index, we store tower height
$h$ at index $-h$, so higher towers are more significant. $\TransMono$ is a linearly ordered
abelian group.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Transseries field]
The \emph{transseries field} is $\TSeries := \mathrm{HahnSeries}(\TransMono, \R)$, a field
by the Hahn-series field instance.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Single transmonomial and one-term transseries]
For $h \in \Z$, $a \in \R$, set $\mono(h,a) := \mathrm{toLex}(\mathrm{single}_{-h}\,a)$, the
transmonomial $(\mathrm{level}\,h)^{a}$, and $\term(h,a) := \mathrm{single}(\mono(h,a))\,1$.
Write $\mathrm{varX}=\term(0,1)=x$, $\mathrm{expX}=\term(1,1)=e^{x}$,
$\mathrm{logX}=\term(-1,1)=\log x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Higher towers dominate]\label{thm:height}
For $h < h'$ and $0 < a'$, and any $a$, $\ \mono(h,a) < \mono(h',a')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By $\mathrm{Finsupp.Lex.lt\_iff}$, strict inequality holds iff there is a least index $i$ of
difference, below which the finsupps agree, with the left strictly smaller at $i$. Take
$i=-h'$: below $-h'$ both single-supported finsupps vanish; at $-h'$ the left is $0$ (support
$-h\neq-h'$) and the right is $a'>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Same-height comparison]\label{thm:same}
For any $h$ and $a < a'$, $\ \mono(h,a) < \mono(h,a')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the criterion at index $-h$: below $-h$ both vanish; at $-h$ the values are $a<a'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exp dominates every power]\label{thm:expdom}
For every real $a$, $\ \mono(0,a) < \mono(1,1)$, i.e.\ $x^{a} \prec e^{x}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:height} with $0<1$, $a'=1>0$. The universality over all real
$a$ is impossible for any single power-series valuation and is the defining feature of
transseries.
\end{proof}

The valuation satisfies $\orderTop(\term(h,a)) = \mono(h,a)$, multiplicativity
$\orderTop(xy)=\orderTop(x)+\orderTop(y)$, and the constant embedding
$\R \hookrightarrow \TSeries$ is injective, so $\R$ is a subfield.

\section{The asymptotic comparison theorem}\label{sec:comparison}

\begin{definition}[Agreement to all orders]
Transseries $a,b$ \emph{agree to all orders} when
\[
  \AgreeToAllOrders(a,b) :\Longleftrightarrow \forall g \in \TransMono,\ (g : \WithTop\,\TransMono) < \orderTop(a-b).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic comparison theorem]\label{thm:compare}
$\AgreeToAllOrders(a,b) \iff a = b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $\orderTop(a-b) \neq \top$, write it as a transmonomial $c$; instantiating
the hypothesis at $g=c$ gives $c<c$, a contradiction. So $\orderTop(a-b)=\top$, whence
$a-b=0$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $a=b$ then $\orderTop(a-b)=\top > g$ for all $g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Agreement to all orders is an equivalence relation, and a nonzero transseries does not agree
to all orders with $0$. Consequently a transseries is uniquely determined by its asymptotic
expansion.
\end{corollary}

The formal order is faithful to real analysis:

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:lo1}
For every $n \in \Nat$, $\ x^{n} = o(e^{x})$ as $x \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:lo2}
For every $n \in \Nat$, $\ (e^{x})^{n} = o(e^{e^{x}})$ as $x \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Compose Theorem~\ref{thm:lo1} with $\exp \to \infty$; this is the analytic shadow of
Theorem~\ref{thm:height} at heights $1<2$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exp-substitution automorphism}\label{sec:expshift}

\begin{definition}[Height shift]
Let $\mathrm{shiftEquiv}\colon \Z \simeq \Z$ be $i \mapsto i-1$. The \emph{height shift}
relabels finsupp indices along $\mathrm{shiftEquiv}$:
$\shift(x) := \mathrm{toLex}(\mathrm{equivMapDomain}\,\mathrm{shiftEquiv}\,(\mathrm{ofLex}\,x))$.
Index $-h$ becomes $-(h+1)$, raising tower height $h$ to $h+1$. The shift is additive and
injective.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exp-substitution preserves dominance]\label{thm:shiftlt}
For all transmonomials $x,y$, $\ \shift(x) < \shift(y) \iff x < y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Unfold with the lexicographic criterion on both sides. A witness index $i$ for $x<y$ yields
the witness $\mathrm{shiftEquiv}(i)$ for $\shift(x) < \shift(y)$: agreement below transports
through $\mathrm{equivMapDomain\_apply}$, and strictness at $i$ transports to
$\mathrm{shiftEquiv}(i)$; the converse uses $\mathrm{shiftEquiv}^{-1}$. Conceptually, a
monotone bijection of the index set maps the least differing index to the least differing
index, so the lexicographic order is preserved. Thus the shift is an order isomorphism of the
value group.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Exp-substitution ring homomorphism]
$\expShift := \mathrm{embDomainRingHom}\ \mathrm{shiftHom}\ \mathrm{shift\_inj}\
\mathrm{shiftHom\_le\_iff} \colon \TSeries \to \TSeries$. The three hypotheses are exactly
additivity, injectivity, and order-reflection (Theorem~\ref{thm:shiftlt}); hence $\expShift$
is a ring homomorphism.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Height shift on a transmonomial]\label{thm:shiftmono}
$\shift(\mono(h,a)) = \mono(h+1,a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides are single-supported; the relabeling sends support index $-h$ to $-(h+1)$,
matching the right side by $\mathrm{single\_apply}$ and case analysis on $-h=i+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exp-substitution on a one-term transseries]\label{thm:expterm}
$\expShift(\term(h,a)) = \term(h+1,a)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reduce via $\mathrm{embDomainRingHom\_apply}$ and $\mathrm{embDomain\_single}$ to
$\mathrm{single}(\shift(\mono(h,a)))\,1 = \mathrm{single}(\mono(h+1,a))\,1$, closed by
Theorem~\ref{thm:shiftmono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Headline]\label{thm:var}
$\expShift(x) = e^{x}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$x=\term(0,1)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:expterm}, $\expShift(\term(0,1))=\term(1,1)=e^{x}$.
\end{proof}

Specializing further: $\expShift(e^{x}) = e^{e^{x}} = \term(2,1)$ and
$\expShift(\log x) = x$.

\begin{theorem}[Fixes the constant field]\label{thm:C}
For every $r \in \R$, $\ \expShift(\C\,r) = \C\,r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\mathrm{embDomainRingHom\_C}$: the value-group map fixes $0$ and constants are supported at
$0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity]\label{thm:inj}
$\expShift$ is injective; it embeds $\TSeries$ into itself.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\mathrm{embDomain\_injective}$, as the value-group map is injective.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:var} and~\ref{thm:C} together show $\expShift$ is a nontrivial field
endomorphism: it moves $x$ to $e^{x}$ while fixing $\R$. Theorem~\ref{thm:shiftlt} is the
entire mathematical content---the order-reflection certifying that $x \mapsto e^{x}$ respects
every asymptotic scale simultaneously.

\section{A worked example}

Consider $t = 2e^{x} + 5x^{3} - 4 + \tfrac12(\log x)^{-1} \in \TSeries$, i.e.\ the
coefficient map $\mono(1,1)\mapsto 2$, $\mono(0,3)\mapsto 5$, $\mono(0,0)\mapsto -4$,
$\mono(-1,-1)\mapsto \tfrac12$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:height} the largest support is
$\mono(1,1)=e^{x}$, so $\orderTop(t)=\mono(1,1)$ and the leading behavior is $2e^{x}$.
Applying $\expShift$ term by term (Theorem~\ref{thm:expterm}) raises each height by one and
fixes coefficients:
\[
  \expShift(t) = 2e^{e^{x}} + 5(e^{x})^{3} - 4 + \tfrac12 x^{-1},
\]
with the constant $-4$ fixed by Theorem~\ref{thm:C}. Finally, if a transseries $s$ agrees
with $t$ to all orders then $\orderTop(t-s)=\top$, so $t-s=0$ and $s=t$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:compare}): the four coefficients are an exact fingerprint.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Lexicographic dominance comparison.} Represent a transmonomial as a finite map
$\text{height}\mapsto\text{exponent}$; scan heights from highest to lowest and decide at the
first difference. Complexity $O(k\log k)$ for $k$ nonzero exponents. Realizes
Theorems~\ref{thm:height}--\ref{thm:expdom}.

\textbf{Exp-substitution (tower shift).} Map every height $h \mapsto h+1$ in every
transmonomial, fixing coefficients. Linear time; correctness by
Theorems~\ref{thm:shiftmono}--\ref{thm:C}; injectivity by Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}.

\textbf{Agreement-to-all-orders test.} Compute $a-b$; it is zero iff (Theorem~\ref{thm:compare})
they agree to all orders. Linear time.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The dominance order decides leading behavior in symbolic limit computation (Gruntz's
algorithm), the comparison theorem guarantees uniqueness of the answer, and the
exp-substitution automorphism models the tower-climbing change of variable in the asymptotics
of ODE solutions, WKB, and resurgence. The order-theoretic core is where transseries
transcend power series: $\mono(0,a) < \mono(1,1)$ for \emph{all} real $a$ is impossible for a
one-dimensional valuation.

\section{Future directions}

Verified companion files establish the law of exponents per tower height, the group
homomorphism $(\R,+)\to\TSeries^{\times}$, value-group unboundedness, and $\mathrm{pow\_var\_lt\_exp}$
(no finite power of $x$ dominates $e^{x}$); the exp-substitution as the automorphism studied
here; and that it is a field \emph{automorphism} (inverse the log-substitution) with cofinal
exp-tower. Open conjectures: valuation scaling
$\orderTop(\expShift\,t)=\WithTop.\mathrm{map}\,\shift\,(\orderTop\,t)$; a derivation making
$\TSeries$ a differential field; an order-embedding of catalog \texttt{FormalTransseries}
evaluation; the tower $\Z$-action $n\mapsto\expShift^{n}$ with
$\expShift^{n}(\term(h,a))=\term(h+n,a)$; and the identification of Archimedean classes with
tower heights.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented a verified field model of single-tower real-power transseries, showed its order
realizes asymptotic dominance (with $e^{x}$ beating every power), proved the asymptotic
comparison theorem, and constructed the exp-substitution automorphism realizing
$x \mapsto e^{x}$ as an injective, scalar-fixing ring homomorphism.

